      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To assess factors that influence pulmonary function, because respiratory system dysfunction is common in chronic spinal cord injury (SCI)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "Cross-sectional cohort study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "Veterans Affairs Boston SCI service and the community."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PARTICIPANTS",
          "content": "Between 1994 and 2003, 339 white men with chronic SCI completed a respiratory questionnaire and underwent spirometry."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTIONS",
          "content": "Not applicable."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "MAIN OUTCOME MEASURES",
          "content": "Forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV(1)), forced vital capacity (FVC), and FEV(1)/FVC."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Adjusting for SCI level and completeness, FEV(1) (-21.0 mL/y; 95% confidence interval [CI], -26.3 to -15.7 mL/y) and FVC (-17.2 mL/y; 95% CI, -23.7 to -10.8 mL/y) declined with age. Lifetime cigarette use was also associated with a decrease in FEV(1) (-3.8 mL/pack-year; 95% CI, -6.5 to -1.1 mL/pack-year), and persistent wheeze and elevated body mass index were associated with a lower FEV(1)/FVC. A greater maximal inspiratory pressure (MIP) was associated with a greater FEV(1) and FVC. FEV(1) significantly decreased with injury duration (-6.1 mL/y; 95% CI, -11.7 to -0.6 mL/y), with the greatest decrement in the most neurologically impaired. The most neurologically impaired also had a greater FEV(1)/FVC, and their FEV(1) and FVC were less affected by age and smoking."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Smoking, persistent wheeze, obesity, and MIP, in addition to SCI level and completeness, were significant determinants of pulmonary function. In SCI, FEV(1), FVC, and FEV(1)/FVC may be less sensitive to factors associated with change in airway size and not reliably detect the severity of airflow obstruction."
        }
      ]},
